/* ============================================================================
bit-count.epher -- how many 1 bits a number has, the popcount

Counts the 1 bits of a value, its population count. The packed while
loop (guide 1.30) peels off the lowest bit with mod(n, 2) and shifts
the rest down one place by halving, exactly a one-bit right shift;
the loop is silent, so labelled prints show the counts. The checks:
255 = 0xff has eight 1 bits (each hex digit f is four ones) and 240 =
0xf0 has four. A recursive def repeats the same count for a whole
list of values.

Source: standard computer arithmetic; the popcount underlies Hamming
weight and error-correcting codes. All values here are small, so the
counts are exact.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/bases/bit-count.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\bases\bit-count.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/bases/bit-count.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

There is no knob: the sample values are the point of the script.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the def, and a list of counts -----------------------------------------------------------
// pop(n) adds the lowest bit, then counts the rest after halving.
def pop(n) = if n == 0 then 0 else mod(n, 2) + pop(floor(n / 2))
for x in {0, 1, 2, 3, 7, 240, 255, 1024} do pop(x)

// ---- the packed while ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// v = {value, count}; each pass drops the lowest bit into the count and
// halves the value (a right shift of one bit).
v = {255, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 2), v[2] + mod(v[1], 2)}
print("popcount(255):", v[2])           // 8: 0xff is eight ones
v = {240, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 2), v[2] + mod(v[1], 2)}
print("popcount(240):", v[2])           // 4: 0xf0 is four ones
print("hex of 240:", hex(240))          // 0xf0: one f, then a zero
v = {1024, 0}
while v[1] > 0 do v = {floor(v[1] / 2), v[2] + mod(v[1], 2)}
print("popcount(1024):", v[2])          // 1: a single bit, 2^10
/* ---- expected output ----
= {0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 1}
= {255, 0}
= popcount(255): 8
= {240, 0}
= popcount(240): 4
= hex of 240: 0xf0
= {1024, 0}
= popcount(1024): 1
*/
